# seph_mcu: the MCU's end of Ledger's SEPROXYHAL link, as bytes.
#
# A Nano X's secure element talks SEPROXYHAL to its MCU over the ISO 7816
# contact once the PPS has set the rate. This block is the MCU: it sends
# SESSION_START on `start`, then on each of its turns -- after every status
# the SE sends -- one event: an HCI "command complete" for a BLE command,
# a STATUS_EVENT for a status request, a BUTTON_PUSH_EVENT when `buttons`
# changes, or a TICKER_EVENT at the SE's interval. That is enough to bring
# an SE up and keep it running; BLE configuration is accepted, not acted on.
# For USB it does the MCU's part -- the bus reset when `usb_plugged`, IN
# completions, the status flags -- and sends a host's own transfers, given
# whole through `inj_*`.
#
# It moves bytes with a valid/ready handshake and knows nothing of ISO 7816;
# `iso7816_uart` carries them on a real contact. README.md has the protocol,
# what was CHECKED on a real SE and what is only QUOTED.
name seph_mcu
version 1.0.0
license MIT
description "Ledger SEPROXYHAL MCU side: session start, turn-based events (ticker, status, button, BLE command complete, USB reset and IN completion, injected host transfers) answering a secure element's commands, over a byte stream"

top seph_mcu
target ice40
target ecp5
target xc7

source rtl/seph_mcu.v

param MS_CYCLES int 112000 1..1000000
param SESSION_LEN int 51 4..64
param STATUS_LEN int 23 4..64
param BUTTON_SHIFT int 1 0..6

port clk in
port rst_n in
port start in
port stop in
port rx_data in 8
port rx_valid in
port tx_data out 8
port tx_valid out
port tx_ready in
port buttons in 2
port usb_plugged in
port usb_on out
port inj_start in
port inj_data in 8
port inj_valid in
port inj_commit in
port inj_busy out
port inj_bad out
port active out
port started out
port rx_packets out 16
port tx_events out 16
port last_rx_tag out 8
port ms out 32
